// The `const ref` argument position for the codepoint distinction. See tests/xfail/identity_char_literal
// for the rule: contextual literal typing is about WIDTH, and `65` is an integer standing in for a
// codepoint rather than the literal written at `char`'s type.
//
// Its own fixture for the same reason as tests/xfail/lit_oob_constref_argument: the `ref` path
// materialises the literal into a temp typed from the PARAMETER (`kama_char`, not the `uint32_t` the arm
// used to write), and that is only sound because an integer never reaches it.
fn void takeC(const ref char c) { }
fn int32 main() {
    takeC(c: 65);
    return 0;
}
